A||||7||Alex2004||Factory Subwoofer question!||||1||
Z||000000||Alex2004||01-14-2001||07:37 PM||||Me and dad are thinking of buying a subwoofer system out of a Wrecked Fiero GT from a guy we know.  Ok, we bought a Sony Xplod CDX 5050, 50watts X 4.  It says not to use another amp without turning the existing amp inside the headunit off.  We have another amp that dad had in his T/A, and i was wondering if i could use it to power the subwoofer it's self and continue using the headunit with full power.  The amp is a cheap wal-mart brand "road gear" and it says it's max output is 250 watts.  So would it be ok to just use the Road Gear Amp to power the subwoofer with the CD unit without turning it's amp off?  I know this sounds confusing but if you know, please tell me!  Thanks...<P>Alex...<p>[This message has been edited by Alex2004 (edited 01-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||01-14-2001||09:16 PM||||If you are going to pull the subsystem, get the overhead slider control, and the factory amp.  That way you can keep all the wiring easy, and be able to use the slider control.  If the box has already been pulled, ask whoever is selling it for the rest of the system.<BR> And if your manual says you must turn off the preamp inside the head unit before connecting another amp, it shouldnt matter what brand amp you're using.  The fact that it is an amp denotes turning off the head unit preamp.  IMHO<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Alex2004||01-14-2001||10:36 PM||||Ok, thanks.  But just to save me time when we get it,  Where is the factory amp located?  And on the top console were the sub switch is at, and i going to have to take the headliner down to get all of the wires down?  And do you know how powerful the Factory amp is?  Let me know, thanks.....<BR>Alex.... BTW what does IMHO stand for?||||reg||4
Z||000003||tgowens||01-15-2001||03:03 AM||||the amp is located inside the right console support panel (behind the carpet against the console on the passenger side of the car.) - it is a 15W amp. The wiring for the switch runs from the radio, behind the dash, up the left A-pillar and under the headliner.<P>IMHO = In My Humble Opinion.......||||reg||1
Z||000004||stimpy||01-15-2001||03:26 PM||||Alex, how much are you gonna be paying for the factory sub?  Have you listened to a car with it in there yet? Reason I ask is that in my opinion the factory sub may not be worth the effort involved if you are expecting anything like boom.<BR>I think it's a lot easier to build a foowell box like I have.  You could probably even run 2 8" woofers. Mine has a 10".  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=258191&a=1921599&p=34099626&Sequence=5&res=high"> <BR>By the way, did you make it to the Fiero club meeting yesterday?  I had to stay home and watch the Raiders get their butts whipped (sigh) so sorry if I missed y'all.<p>[This message has been edited by stimpy (edited 01-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||01-15-2001||04:12 PM||||Umm. Maybe I misunderstood, but did you say you were going to power the factory sub with a 250W amp? I would call it factory toast! No way 250W will work with the factory sub. I would refrain from that idea, and use an aftermarket setup like Stimpy's idea. It will sound tons better. The factory system will give you about as much bass as a pair of 6x9's. There is a website that gives a good description of how to build and install a ported enclosure for an 8" sub and mount in the stock location. Its really very easy. I would reccomend you use that or Stimpy's idea, or even mine (10" in bandpass box in the front truck). I wouldnt waste your money on a stock setup.<P>P.S. You will be okay to use the headunit like that, just dont run a speaker output from the headunit into an RCA(Preamp) input on the amp. That will also creat some not so tasty toast.<BR>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/transsig.gif">     <BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto (3.4 Conversion in Progress)<BR><I>"It's always in the last place you look." Of course it is. Why would you keep looking after you've found it? Do people do this? Who and where are they?</I><p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 01-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex2004||01-15-2001||07:30 PM||||I'm not sure how much we are paying for it.  Well i was kinda expecting alittle bomb, do you think if i get it i could just wired it up to a regular speaker wire since that would still make it more the 15 watts.  Since my car is an 84 it has two sets of wires for the driver and the passenger and we took the 84 seats out and cut holes in the frame where the 86 + speakers go, do you think i could just wired it up to one of the spare speaker wires?  If i did that could i still use the control on the overhead console?  LMK, thanks!<P>Alex... BTW stimpy, we didn't make it this time, we were busy.  We'll most likely be there next time! <p>[This message has been edited by Alex2004 (edited 01-15-2001).]||||reg||4
Z||000007||286SE'S||01-17-2001||02:24 PM||||Expect that all you are getting is a nice plastic box that fits up in the dash. The speaker is 15 years old and is probably no good. I have tried what you suggest. Running the Factory subwoofer from a speaker output. It sounded awful. Not really bass but a honking sound. The factory amp has a crossover in it to allow just the very low frequencies to be amplified. The overhead control cannot be used with another amp it is  not a fader. It changes the voltage applied to the amps gain circuit. Rodney Dickman sells a replacement speaker that is better than the original. Fuller bass, less distortion. He spent a lot of time to find the right speaker and has done a great job.<BR>You should either go with the whole factory system for "original equipment" sake or just put in whatever you want for the sound quality. You can't have both.||||reg||1
